["Marketing litres versus what a family actually fits inside",
"A single-drawer machine stated at 9-10 litres and a dual-basket machine stated at 10.4 litres combined, two baskets of 5.2 litres each, sound equivalent on paper but behave very differently in practice. The single large drum can take a whole chicken or a full tray of vegetables in one continuous space; the split machine has two smaller compartments, each capped by its own diameter, so a whole chicken over roughly 1.6kg often won't fit in either basket even though the combined litre figure looks larger. Basket diameter, not total volume, is what determines whether a whole joint or a full family-sized tray fits, and manufacturers rarely publish it. Check product photos for a chicken, a pizza, or a comparable reference object shown inside the actual basket before trusting the litre number on its own."],
